# A kernel that fails after frames have been staged (D[async-output],
# #113). Once a kernel has failed, the driver returns the error for every
# event, also for those whose copies had arrived before, so the runtime
# marks a slot's copies as arrived with a function of the host that the
# stream of copies runs after them; and since the runtime ends the process
# with abort at a failure of the driver, it first has the thread of the
# frames write those whose copies had arrived. Here
# MDRT_FAULT_AFTER_FRAMES=2 makes a kernel trap after the copies of the
# 2nd frame (step 20) have arrived, while the thread of the frames holds
# each write back by 100 ms: both frames are still queued when the run meets
# the failure, and the ring of 3 slots is not full, so nothing else waits
# for them. They are written, as a run whose frames had no thread wrote them
# before the failure, the frames of the reference to the byte, and the run
# ends with the runtime's report of the failure, once, not with a failure
# of the copy of a frame.
#
# REQUIRES: cuda
#
# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
# RUN: sed -e 's|Inputs/|%S/Inputs/|' \
# RUN:     -e 's|energy_interval = 50|energy_interval = 10\ntrajectory = "run.dcd"\ntrajectory_interval = 10|' \
# RUN:     %S/mixture.toml > %t/run.toml
# RUN: printf '[execution]\ntarget = "GPU"\ndeterministic = true\n' >> %t/run.toml
# RUN: sed -e 's|run.dcd|plain.dcd|' %t/run.toml > %t/plain.toml
# RUN: cd %t && mdir run plain.toml > /dev/null
# RUN: cd %t && env MDIR_FRAME_WRITE_DELAY=100 MDRT_FAULT_AFTER_FRAMES=2 not --crash mdir run run.toml > out 2> err
# RUN: FileCheck %s < %t/err
# RUN: grep -c 'failed with CUDA_ERROR' %t/err | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=ONCE
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/dcd_prefix.py %t/run.dcd %t/plain.dcd | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FRAMES

# CHECK-NOT: the copy of the frame
# CHECK: mdrt: {{.*}} failed with CUDA_ERROR_LAUNCH_FAILED
# CHECK-NOT: the copy of the frame
# ONCE: {{^1$}}
# FRAMES: 2 frames, 2 in the header, the first of the reference
